NEW_DOCS = {'1996-resilio-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding-stale-predecessor-handle-retirement-and-pointer-fragmentation-evaluation.md': "# Resilio remedy hardening attestation successor reference rebinding, stale predecessor handle retirement, and pointer fragmentation evaluation\n\n## What current Resilio gets right\n\nCurrent official Resilio materials still deserve credit for being candid that `a successor world exists`, `the old world broke`, and `all practical references now safely point at the successor` are not one flat truth.\nThat candor is useful.\n\nThe strongest present ingredients are:\n\n- current `If your device is stolen` docs still say the serious response path regenerates identity, relinks devices, and reshares folders, which implicitly acknowledges that predecessor references cannot simply be assumed to remain valid\n- current `Sync Service Troubleshooting on Windows` docs still say switching to Local System creates a new storage world with no old folders visible and requires re-add plus re-share or reconnect\n- current `Link structure and flow` docs still say folder links contain folder name, folder ID, temporary key, expiration, and client version, and that they are handed from browser to app\n- current `Sync Share Dialog (Desktop)` docs still say sharing links can be copied to clipboard or inserted into email or messenger, which means references propagate beyond one UI surface\n- current `Sharing single file` docs still say a file link can be made non-expiring, has no usage-limit or device-ban control, and recipients can share files further\n- current `Key structure and flow` docs still say changing a Standard-folder key is not automatically distributed and peers with the old key continue syncing with each other\n- current `What's the difference between Standard and Advanced folders?` docs still say Standard-folder peers can share their keys without limitation and that Standard cannot be upgraded in place to Advanced\n\n## Where the current contract still fragments\n\nThe problem is not that Resilio lacks clues about successor rebinding.\nThe problem is that it still does not produce one first-class, case-scoped **reference rebinding and predecessor-handle retirement** object.\n\nToday an operator can often infer only weaker truths such as:\n\n- the successor world works, but old links, keys, bookmarks, or copied share messages may still point at predecessor-era handles\n- one group has been re-shared or reconnected, but there is no single page proving which references were rebound and which stale ones remain live\n- a Standard-folder key may have changed for one peer while old-key peers keep syncing among themselves, yet the product does not elevate that into a pointer-divergence receipt\n- a single-file link may have been sent outward and can remain non-expiring and re-shareable, but there is no typed case object showing whether that predecessor pointer was retired, replaced, or left outside governance\n- a new identity or new storage world may be real, but the operator still has to reconstruct which names, IDs, and handles now canonically represent the successor\n\nThose are useful clues.\nThey are not the same as an explicit answer to `which predecessor references still resolve, which are tombstoned, which are rebound to the successor, who was informed of the canonical pointer, and which stronger discovery or reachability sentence must remain blocked?`\n\n## Why that matters for AnonSync\n\nAnonSync needs a stronger sentence than `the successor is legitimate`.\nIt needs to support claims such as:\n\n- the successor world is legitimate, but only a named subset of predecessor handles have been retired\n- the successor world is canonical for named slices, while old user-facing references remain live and dangerous\n- old handles are blocked internally, but off-world copied links remain outside governance\n- key rotation or receipt reissue happened, but dependent parties were not yet all rebound to the successor pointer\n\nSo AnonSync should not clone Resilio's present contract at this seam.\nIt should borrow the candor about identity regeneration, re-share and reconnect requirements, explicit link and key structure, non-expiring outward links, and old-key continuation, while replacing the fragmented operator story with one first-class family for **successor reference rebinding, stale predecessor handle retirement, and canonical-pointer truth**.\n", '1997-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding-contract-sheet-page-predecessor-handle-set-canonical-pointer-and-retirement-budget-interface-spec.md': '# Remedy-hardening-attestation successor reference rebinding contract sheet page — predecessor handle set, canonical pointer, and retirement budget\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis page is the compact contract for deciding whether a legitimate successor world has also become the canonical place that dependent humans and systems are meant to reach.\nIt exists so the product can distinguish `successor exists`, `successor inherits standing`, `some references rebound`, `old handles still live`, `canonical pointer published for named slice`, `predecessor handles tombstoned for named slice`, and `broader discovery continuity still blocked`.\n\n## Core fields\n\n- successor-reference-rebinding identifier\n- source successor-inheritance receipt identifier\n- predecessor-world identifier\n- successor-world identifier\n- governed slice identifier\n- predecessor handle family under review\n- predecessor handle inventory\n- predecessor handle exposure class\n- successor canonical pointer class\n- successor canonical handle set\n- handle-resolution rulebook version\n- bridge-authority or rebinding authority source\n- internal pointer-retirement status\n- external pointer-retirement status\n- copied-link or copied-key outside-governance status\n- share-message or bookmark propagation status\n- stale-handle discovery status\n- tombstone publication status\n- redirect or successor-resolution status\n- dependent-party rebinding notice status\n- current successor-reference-rebinding class\n- highest currently safe rebinding sentence\n- strongest blocked stronger sentence\n- next fact that upgrades rebinding standing now\n- next fact that collapses rebinding standing now\n\n## Successor-reference-rebinding classes\n\nThe page must model at least these distinct classes:\n\n- successor legitimate, rebinding unreviewed\n- successor canonical pointer chosen, predecessor handles still live\n- internal references rebound, copied external references unknown\n- predecessor handles tombstoned for named slice only\n- successor redirect live for named slice only\n- stale predecessor handles still resolving\n- rebinding notice sent, acknowledgement partial\n- named handle family retired and successor pointer canonical for named slice only\n- broader universal discovery continuity blocked\n\n## Minimum required comparisons\n\nThe page must force explicit comparison between:\n\n- successor legitimacy and successor discoverability\n- canonical successor pointer and surviving predecessor handles\n- internal retirement and external retirement\n- redirect success and tombstone success\n- chosen canonical handle and actually propagated handle\n- handle retirement and dependent-party rebinding acknowledgement\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- `the successor world is valid` must never silently upgrade to `everyone now reaches it through the right handle`.\n- `we re-shared or reconnected` must never silently upgrade to `all predecessor links or keys are retired`.\n- `a new key was issued` must never silently upgrade to `all old keys stopped mattering`.\n- `the new world has the same name or path` must never silently upgrade to `old references now safely mean the new world`.\n- `some redirects work` must never silently upgrade to `all stale discovery vectors are closed`.\n', '1998-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding-review-page-do-all-references-now-point-to-the-successor-and-which-predecessor-handles-still-remain-live-interface-spec.md': '# Remedy-hardening-attestation successor reference rebinding review page — do all references now point to the successor, and which predecessor handles still remain live?\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis page is the operator-facing review that answers the practical aftermath question after successor-world inheritance is already understood: even if the successor may legitimately carry standing, do dependent humans and systems now reach it through the correct canonical handles, or do predecessor handles still remain live, copied, or ambiguous?\n\n## Primary review prompts\n\nThe review must answer these prompts in order:\n\n1. **Which successor-inheritance receipt is the starting basis for this rebinding review?**\n2. **Which predecessor handle families are in scope: links, keys, bookmarks, copied messages, approval prompts, user-facing names, API or export references, or local path aliases?**\n3. **What handle now counts as the canonical successor pointer for the named slice?**\n4. **Which predecessor handles were tombstoned, which were redirected, which were reissued, and which remain uncontrolled?**\n5. **Who has been notified of the canonical successor pointer, and who still appears to rely on predecessor-era references?**\n6. **What is the strongest successor-reference-rebinding sentence the product may honestly publish now?**\n\n## Review sections\n\n### 1. Predecessor-handle basis board\n\nShow:\n\n- source successor-inheritance receipt\n- predecessor world identifier\n- successor world identifier\n- predecessor handle families in scope\n- strongest honest inheritance sentence before rebinding review\n\n### 2. Canonical-pointer board\n\nShow:\n\n- chosen canonical successor handle\n- whether it is internal only, external only, or both\n- whether it resolves for all intended audiences\n- whether redirects or tombstones exist for predecessor handles\n\n### 3. Live-stale-handle board\n\nShow:\n\n- predecessor handles still live internally\n- predecessor handles still live externally\n- copied link and copied key exposure\n- same-name or same-path ambiguity exposure\n- old-key continuation exposure\n\n### 4. Rebinding-notice board\n\nShow:\n\n- who was told about the successor pointer\n- who acknowledged the rebinding\n- who still appears to act on stale handles\n- who is out of governance entirely\n\n### 5. Sentence chooser\n\nThe review must output one and only one primary sentence class such as:\n\n- successor inheritance stands, but rebinding has not yet been reviewed\n- canonical successor pointer chosen, predecessor handles still live\n- internal handles rebound, external copied references uncontrolled\n- predecessor handles tombstoned for named slice only\n- redirects live for named slice only, universal discovery still blocked\n- named handle families retired and successor pointer canonical for named slice only\n- stale predecessor handles still resolve, so stronger discovery claims remain blocked\n\n## Hard rules\n\nThe review must never let an operator hide:\n\n- stale links behind `reshared`\n- old-key continuity behind `rotated`\n- copied outward messages behind `canonical pointer chosen`\n- same name or same path behind `successor discovered`\n- partial rebinding behind `everyone now points to the new world`\n', '1999-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding-proof-page-handle-ledger-resolution-map-and-rebinding-ceiling-interface-spec.md': '# Remedy-hardening-attestation successor reference rebinding proof page — handle ledger, resolution map, and rebinding ceiling\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis page is the evidence-heavy proof surface for determining whether predecessor-era references have actually been retired, redirected, or rebound to the legitimate successor world.\nIt proves the ceiling on any claim that tries to say the successor is not only valid, but also the canonical target reached through the right handles.\n\n## Required evidence blocks\n\n### 1. Predecessor-handle ledger\n\nPreserve:\n\n- predecessor world identifier\n- predecessor handle families\n- handle issuance source\n- handle propagation surfaces\n- handle current live or copied status\n\n### 2. Successor-pointer ledger\n\nPreserve:\n\n- successor world identifier\n- chosen canonical successor handle set\n- handle audience scope\n- handle publication channels\n- handle reissue events\n\n### 3. Retirement and redirect ledger\n\nPreserve:\n\n- predecessor handles explicitly tombstoned\n- predecessor handles redirected to successor\n- predecessor handles still resolving as predecessor\n- predecessor handles outside governance\n- redirect or tombstone effective-scope witnesses\n\n### 4. Dependent rebinding ledger\n\nPreserve:\n\n- dependent humans and systems notified\n- acknowledgements received\n- stale-handle hits detected\n- successful successor-handle resolutions observed\n- still-ambiguous consumers\n\n### 5. Contradiction and downgrade ledger\n\nPreserve:\n\n- old-key peer continuity exposure\n- non-expiring shared-link exposure\n- copied message or bookmark exposure\n- same-name alias ambiguity exposure\n- conflicting resolution witnesses\n- resulting downgrade class\n\n### 6. Claim ceiling\n\nRender:\n\n- highest honest canonical-pointer sentence\n- highest honest tombstone sentence\n- highest honest redirect sentence\n- highest honest dependent-rebinding sentence\n- blocked stronger sentence and exact blocker\n\n## Evidence classes\n\nThe page must distinguish at least:\n\n- successor legitimate only\n- canonical successor pointer chosen only\n- internal handles rebound only\n- predecessor handles tombstoned for named slice only\n- redirects active for named slice only\n- old handles still live\n- dependent rebinding partial\n- external copied references uncontrolled\n- universal discovery continuity blocked\n\n## Hard rules\n\nThe proof page must never treat:\n\n- re-share success as proof all predecessor handles are retired\n- key rotation as proof old keys stopped resolving\n- same visible folder name as proof that stale references now mean the successor\n- redirect for one channel as proof that every audience reaches the successor\n- calm after rebuild as proof that predecessor discovery vectors disappeared\n', '2000-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding-timeline-page-break-reissue-rebind-deprecate-and-tombstone-events-interface-spec.md': '# Remedy-hardening-attestation successor reference rebinding timeline page — break, reissue, rebind, deprecate, and tombstone events\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis page is the ordered timeline for understanding how predecessor-era references were handled after a world break, successor ratification, or forced re-foundation.\nIt exists so later operators can see whether the successor merely became legitimate, or whether the surrounding reference ecosystem was actually repaired.\n\n## Required event families\n\nThe timeline must be able to render at least these event families:\n\n- predecessor handle issued\n- predecessor handle propagated outward\n- catastrophe or world break detected\n- successor world created\n- inheritance bridge ratified\n- canonical successor pointer selected\n- replacement key or link issued\n- predecessor handle marked stale\n- predecessor handle tombstoned\n- predecessor handle redirected to successor\n- dependent rebinding notice sent\n- stale-handle hit observed\n- acknowledgement of successor pointer received\n- old-key or old-link continuity detected\n- handle family declared outside governance\n- canonical-pointer sentence upgraded or downgraded\n\n## Event cards\n\nEvery event card must preserve:\n\n- event identifier\n- event class\n- timestamp\n- affected governed slice\n- predecessor handle family\n- resulting successor-reference-rebinding class\n- rebinding effect\n- blocker created or removed\n- manual step required\n- resulting stronger sentence blocked or unlocked\n\n## Fixed rendering order\n\nEvery successor-reference-rebinding timeline must render the same sections in the same order:\n\n1. **Predecessor handle basis**\n2. **Break and successor-world basis**\n3. **Canonical-pointer selection and handle reissue**\n4. **Retirement, redirect, and stale-hit outcomes**\n5. **Dependent rebinding status and final sentence**\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- The timeline must never hide predecessor-handle survival behind later successor legitimacy.\n- The timeline must never hide reissue behind `same reference`.\n- The timeline must never hide stale-handle hits behind `mostly migrated`.\n- The timeline must never let later calm erase the fact that old references remained live for some horizon.\n', '2001-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding-lineage-receipt-page-canonical-successor-pointer-retired-handles-and-blocked-stronger-discovery-sentences-interface-spec.md': '# Remedy-hardening-attestation successor reference rebinding lineage receipt page — canonical successor pointer, retired handles, and blocked stronger discovery sentences\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis page is the durable receipt for the strongest honest successor-reference-rebinding sentence after reviewing whether predecessor-era handles still remain live or ambiguous.\nIt is the page a later operator opens when they need to know which handle now canonically represents the successor, which predecessor handles were retired or redirected, who was told, and which stronger discovery sentence remained blocked.\n\n## Required receipt fields\n\nThe receipt must preserve:\n\n- successor-reference-rebinding identifier\n- source successor-inheritance receipt identifier\n- predecessor world identifier\n- successor world identifier\n- named governed slice\n- current successor-reference-rebinding class\n- predecessor handle families in scope\n- canonical successor handle set\n- predecessor handles tombstoned status\n- predecessor handles redirected status\n- predecessor handles still live status\n- external copied-reference status\n- dependent rebinding notice and acknowledgement status\n- bridge-authority or rebinding-authority source\n- rulebook version\n- highest honest successor-reference-rebinding sentence\n- strongest blocked stronger sentence\n- exact blocker summary\n- next evidence needed for upgrade\n- next evidence that would force downgrade\n- issuance timestamp\n- issuer identity\n\n## Canonical sentence families\n\nThe receipt must support at least these sentence families:\n\n- the successor world is legitimate, but rebinding has not yet been reviewed\n- a canonical successor pointer is chosen for the named slice, while predecessor handles still remain live\n- predecessor handles are retired internally for the named slice, while copied external references remain uncontrolled\n- redirects to the successor are active for the named slice only\n- stale predecessor handles still resolve, so stronger discovery claims remain blocked\n- named handle families are retired and the successor pointer is canonical for the named slice only\n- broader universal discovery continuity remains blocked\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- The receipt must never collapse `successor inherited standing` into `everyone now reaches the successor through the right handle`.\n- The receipt must never collapse `reissued` into `same reference still valid`.\n- The receipt must never collapse `old key replaced here` into `old key stopped mattering everywhere`.\n- The receipt must never collapse `same name` into `same canonical pointer`.\n- The receipt must never collapse `named handle family retired` into `all predecessor discovery vectors closed`.\n'}

for name, content in NEW_DOCS.items():
    (DOCS / name).write_text(content, encoding="utf-8")

README_ADD = "\n\n## Revision addendum after rev0475 — successor reference rebinding, stale predecessor handle retirement, and canonical-pointer ceiling\n\nThis continuation archive advances the doctrine by tightening another concrete non-clone seam around **what happens after a successor world is already legitimate enough to inherit bounded standing, but the product still has to decide whether people and systems now actually reach it through the right handles instead of stale predecessor links, keys, copied messages, bookmarks, names, or local aliases**.\nIt does eight things in one tranche:\n\n1. Continues the archive after rev0475 with a new page family centered on what happens *after* successor-world inheritance legitimacy exists but *before* the product should pretend that discovery, reachability, and canonical reference truth automatically followed.\n2. Tightens the non-clone line again: borrow Resilio's candor about regenerated identities, Local System storage-world forks that require re-add and re-share or reconnect, explicit link structure, copied and messaged share links, non-expiring single-file links with no usage cap, Standard-key unlimited resharing, and old-key continuity after key changes; refuse any contract where the operator still has to reconstruct `which predecessor handles still resolve, which are tombstoned, and which pointer is actually canonical now?` from scattered support articles.\n3. Adds one new **Resilio evaluation** document focused on why current remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding truth is still too fragmented to clone even though the ingredients are useful.\n4. Adds five new **interface specs** for remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding contract sheet, review, proof, timeline, and lineage receipt.\n5. Makes one hard product decision explicit: **successor-world inheritance legitimacy is weaker than successor reference rebinding legitimacy.**\n6. Makes another hard product decision explicit: **canonical successor pointer chosen is weaker than predecessor-handle retirement proven.**\n7. Makes a third hard product decision explicit: **`re-shared`, `reconnected`, `same name`, `new key issued`, and `successor works` are never allowed to impersonate universal pointer rebinding or stale-handle retirement.**\n8. Packages the result as another continuation archive whose new tranche makes the `predecessor handle / successor canonical pointer / redirect versus tombstone / copied-link exposure / rebinding notice / blocked stronger discovery sentence` seam explicit in the reading order and page family.\n\nNew docs in this tranche:\n\n- `1996-resilio-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding-stale-predecessor-handle-retirement-and-pointer-fragmentation-evaluation.md`\n- `1997-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding-contract-sheet-page-predecessor-handle-set-canonical-pointer-and-retirement-budget-interface-spec.md`\n- `1998-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding-review-page-do-all-references-now-point-to-the-successor-and-which-predecessor-handles-still-remain-live-interface-spec.md`\n- `1999-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding-proof-page-handle-ledger-resolution-map-and-rebinding-ceiling-interface-spec.md`\n- `2000-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding-timeline-page-break-reissue-rebind-deprecate-and-tombstone-events-interface-spec.md`\n- `2001-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding-lineage-receipt-page-canonical-successor-pointer-retired-handles-and-blocked-stronger-discovery-sentences-interface-spec.md`\n"
STATUS_ADD = '\n\n## Revision addendum — successor reference rebinding, stale predecessor handle retirement, and canonical-pointer ceiling after rev0475\n\nThe next seam after **successor-world inheritance bridge legitimacy and bounded carry-forward** is now explicit:\n\n- the archive can already say whether a successor world exists, which predecessor families it may inherit, which claims had to be reissued, and which stronger continuity sentence stayed blocked\n- it still needed to own the harder truth of **whether predecessor-era references now actually point to the successor, which stale handles remain live, which were redirected or tombstoned, and who still relies on predecessor pointers**, because successor legitimate, successor canonical, some handles rebound, internal handles retired, external copied references uncontrolled, stale key still live, redirects active, and universal discovery continuity blocked are not one consequence class\n- current Resilio docs reinforce that gap because stolen-device recovery requires regenerated identity and resharing, Local System service mode creates a new storage world that requires re-add and re-share or reconnect, links and share messages travel through browser, clipboard, email, and messenger, single-file links can be non-expiring and re-shareable, Standard-folder peers can share keys without limitation, and Standard-key changes do not distribute automatically while old-key peers keep syncing among themselves\n\nThis pass turns that gap into a first-class product object: **the remedy-hardening-attestation successor reference rebinding and stale predecessor handle retirement case**.\n\nWhat is newly true in the archive:\n\n- **successor-world inheritance legitimacy is weaker than successor reference rebinding legitimacy**\n- **successor legitimate only, canonical successor pointer chosen, internal handles rebound, stale predecessor handles still live, redirects active, named handle family tombstoned, copied external references uncontrolled, and universal discovery continuity blocked are separate public truths**\n- **legitimate successor is weaker than discoverable successor, discoverable successor is weaker than predecessor-handle retirement, and predecessor-handle retirement is weaker than universal rebinding proof**\n- **re-shared folders, reconnected peers, same visible names, and freshly issued keys now degrade into rebinding inputs instead of impersonating successor-pointer closure**\n- **every serious successor-reference sentence now needs one receipt that preserves predecessor handle families, canonical successor handle, retired versus redirected handles, copied-reference exposure, dependent rebinding status, highest honest rebinding sentence, and the blocked stronger discovery sentence**\n\nWhat remains intentionally true:\n\n- successor inheritance still matters\n- predecessor-versus-successor bridge legitimacy still matters\n- same-world continuity still matters\n- but none of those may substitute for one explicit answer about which old references still resolve, which were retired, and whether the successor is actually the canonical thing dependent parties now reach\n'
EVAL_ADD = "\n\n## Revision addendum after rev0475 — why successor reference rebinding, stale predecessor handle retirement, and canonical-pointer truth now sit on the non-clone side\n\nCurrent official Resilio docs are still admirably candid that `the successor world is valid`, `the successor works`, `the old references are gone`, and `everyone now reaches the right successor pointer` are not one flat truth.\n`If your device is stolen` still says the serious response path regenerates identity, relinks devices, and reshares folders, explicitly breaking the old instance rather than pretending its references silently survive.\n`Sync Service Troubleshooting on Windows` still says switching to Local System creates a new storage world with no old folders visible and requires re-add plus re-share or reconnect.\n`Link structure and flow` still says folder links contain folder name, folder ID, temporary key, expiration, and client version and are handed from browser to app.\n`Sync Share Dialog (Desktop)` still says sharing links can be copied to clipboard or inserted into email or messenger.\n`Sharing single file` still says file links can be made non-expiring, have no usage-limit or device-ban control, and recipients can share the files further.\n`What's the difference between Standard and Advanced folders?` still says Standard peers can share the key they have without limitation.\n`Key structure and flow` still says changing a Standard-folder key is not distributed automatically and peers with the old key continue syncing with each other, while no longer syncing with the peer that changed the key.\n\nThis is strong operational candor.\nIt is also exactly why AnonSync should not clone the present contract.\nOne ordinary answer to `which predecessor links, keys, copied share messages, names, bookmarks, and handles still resolve, which now canonically represent the successor, and which stronger discovery sentence must remain blocked?` still depends on combining reset guidance, service-world forks, link anatomy, share-dialog behavior, non-expiring outward links, Standard-key resharing rules, and old-key continuation semantics.\n\nSo this tranche freezes a stronger replacement line: **legitimate successor only, canonical successor pointer chosen, internal rebinding only, named tombstones active, redirects active, stale predecessor handles still live, external copied references uncontrolled, and universal discovery continuity blocked become separate modeled truths.**\n\nThat is why this revision adds five more first-class pages: **Remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding contract sheet**, **successor-reference-rebinding review**, **successor-reference-rebinding proof**, **successor-reference-rebinding timeline**, and **successor-reference-rebinding lineage receipt**.\n"
SOURCES_ADD = "\n\n## rev0476 source set — successor reference rebinding, stale predecessor handle retirement, and canonical-pointer ceiling\n\nThe most load-bearing source set for this pass was:\n\n- Resilio's current `If your device is stolen` article, which still says the serious response path regenerates identity, relinks devices, and reshares folders, explicitly breaking the old instance.\n- Resilio's current `Sync Service Troubleshooting on Windows` article, which still says switching to Local System creates a new storage world with no old folders visible and requires re-add plus re-share or reconnect.\n- Resilio's current `Link structure and flow` article, which still says folder links contain folder name, folder ID, temporary key, expiration, and client version and are handed from browser to app.\n- Resilio's current `Sync Share Dialog (Desktop)` article, which still says sharing links can be copied to clipboard or inserted into email or messenger.\n- Resilio's current `Sharing single file` article, which still says single-file links can be made non-expiring, have no usage-limit or device-ban control, and recipients can share the files further.\n- Resilio's current `What's the difference between Standard and Advanced folders?` article, which still says Standard peers can share the key they have without limitation and that Standard folders cannot be upgraded in place to Advanced folders.\n- Resilio's current `Key structure and flow` article, which still says changing a Standard-folder key is not distributed automatically and that peers with the old key continue syncing with each other.\n\nThose sources were enough to tighten the line again:\n\n- current Resilio still deserves credit for candid successor-pointer ingredients\n- but current Resilio still answers `which old references still matter, and which one is canonical now?` too diffusely\n- AnonSync should therefore prefer explicit remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-reference-rebinding sheets, reviews, proofs, timelines, and lineage receipts over overloaded reset guides, link anatomy notes, share-dialog behavior, and Standard-key lore\n\nPrimary sources:\n\n- If your device is stolen\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/204644049-If-your-device-is-stolen\n\n- Sync Service Troubleshooting on Windows\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/207724196-Sync-Service-Troubleshooting-on-Windows\n\n- Link structure and flow\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/204754739-Link-structure-and-flow\n\n- Sync Share Dialog (Desktop)\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/204790709-Sync-Share-Dialog-Desktop\n\n- Sharing single file\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/115000401010-Sharing-single-file\n\n- What's the difference between Standard and Advanced folders?\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/204754679-What-s-the-difference-between-Standard-and-Advanced-folders\n\n- Key structure and flow\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/206767810-Key-structure-and-flow\n"

(ROOT / "README.md").write_text((ROOT / "README.md").read_text(encoding="utf-8") + README_ADD, encoding="utf-8")
